WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.270
The tension in the air was palpable.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:07.720
Everyone sat around the long oak table was
sweating in their business suits, tugging

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:12.450
at their ties so that it might dislodge the
collective lumps in everyone’s throats.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:16.970
At the head of the table, someone sat facing
away from the rest of the gathered executives,

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:22.550
their silk-gloved hands interlocked, the crown
of a top hat just visible over the back of

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.520
their broad, throne-like chair.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:30.240
“So, my friends,” they said without turning
around, in a voice that filled the boardroom,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.870
“What a sorry state of affairs this is.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:41.149
The SCP Foundation sticking their noses in,
sniffling around the company, shutting down

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:42.149
factories.

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:46.239
And then there are our customers, too obsessed
with Fortnite to be interested in the toys

00:00:46.239 --> 00:00:47.239
we’re making.

00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:51.460
If Wondertainment Enterprises can't touch
the hearts of every man, woman, and child

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.519
on the planet, then what are we even doing
here?

00:00:54.519 --> 00:00:56.250
What’s our point?”

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:57.920
A pause lingered.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.670
Nobody in the room knew whether to answer
or keep quiet and let somebody else speak

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:03.670
first.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.860
“That wasn’t a rhetorical question!”

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.189
the voice yelled from its chair.

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:12.159
“We are a company that makes toys.

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:14.750
That is our point, and that’s why you’re
here.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.780
So, go ahead, don’t be shy.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.770
Pitch me something.”

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:23.189
After another moment of silence, one of the
board members nervously stood up from his

00:01:23.189 --> 00:01:24.189
chair.

00:01:24.189 --> 00:01:28.560
Attempting to straighten his tie, he produced
a box containing a prototype.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.740
“Uh, I might have something, Doctor,”
he said, nervously struggling to compose himself.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.250
“Go on.”

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:39.810
Opening the box, the man produced a small
circular, spinning top with jagged edges – or

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.621
a Beyblade, to those born after 1999.

00:01:41.621 --> 00:01:48.950
“So, nineties nostalgia is b-back in right
now,” the executive stammered, assembling

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.909
the Beyblade in his shaking hands.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:55.180
“And Wondertainment Industries can be the
first to relaunch this popular playground

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:56.979
craze from back in the day.”

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:00.740
He pulled on the ripcord, letting the spinning
toy loose on the desk.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.880
Instead of spiraling in a circle though, the
Beyblade shot across the table’s surface

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.370
and hopped down to the floor, a tiny voice
emanating from it the entire time.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.340
“No, no, please don’t make me fight!

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:12.340
I can’t.

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:13.340
I won’t do it!

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:17.900
I don’t want to die,” it sobbed as it
raced across the floor and out of the door.

00:02:17.900 --> 00:02:22.050
Realizing he didn’t need to say anything
else, the board member sat back down.

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:25.160
Another stood up, more eager now that somebody
else had gone first.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.000
“Well, we were thinking something more for
the younger market,” she declared.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.450
She produced a large flashcard that displayed
an image of a pretty ordinary-looking hobby

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.450
horse.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.440
“You can’t really go wrong with a classic,
right?”

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:40.200
she asked, looking around the room for any
looks of approval from the rest of the board.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.190
“Anyway, these hobby horses are durable.

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:43.960
They come in a range of colors…

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:46.800
there’s just, um, one little caveat.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.980
We got some calls from the Italian Prime Minister
after we’d been testing them.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.630
Apparently, at the exact same time someone
rocks on one of these hobby horses, a random

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.510
person in Italy gets turned to dust.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:01.030
We currently have… um, no way of counteracting
this.”

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.010
There were a few short murmurs from the rest
of the board.

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:08.200
Sensing she was losing them, the same executive
signaled her assistant, who retrieved something

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.870
from outside the boardroom.

00:03:09.870 --> 00:03:14.310
As she re-entered, she was holding a teddy
bear at arm’s length, grimacing in disgust.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:19.040
The rest of the board quickly realized why;
the bear carried with it a pungent stench

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.940
that hung heavy in the air.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.660
“Keeping with that younger market I was
talking about,” the exec piped up again,

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:26.840
holding her nose shut.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.950
“Kids love gross-out humor, right?

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.460
You put a fart in an animated movie, and the
tykes go nuts for it.

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:37.560
So, this… delightful product – ‘the
Stinky Bear’ – can bring that same enjoyment

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.290
into the home for the low price of $39.99!”

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:45.460
“Are those the same bears that R&amp;D was testing
a while back?” one of the other Wondertainment

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:46.460
executives asked.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.480
“The ones that had a one in five chance
of releasing lethal amounts of Novichok?”

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.320
“Wait, isn’t that a Russian nerve agent?”

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:54.320
another exclaimed in horror.

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.370
“Get that Stinky Bear out of here!”

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.470
Defeated, the presenting executive retook
her seat while her assistant carried the bear

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.800
to the incinerator.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:06.790
“Well, this has been a poor show thus far,”
came the good doctor’s voice from the far

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.530
end of the table, interrupted by a tinny,
electronic beeping.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:14.040
“Mr. Glenridge, what is that in your pocket?”

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.870
“Oh, sorry, feeding time,” the board member,
Mr. Glenridge, apologized, pulling out a tiny,

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.110
oval-shaped device and, oddly enough, a candy
bar.

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:23.410
“It’s a Tamagotchi.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:24.590
You remember those, right?

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:29.570
Well, we whipped up this one to be more intelligent,
but it keeps demanding actual, solid food,

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.180
so I just have to…”

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:35.350
Glenridge then proceeded to tear open the
candy wrapper and begin mushing it into the

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.260
Tamagotchi’s LCD screen.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:41.310
The virtual pet made a number of sad-sounding
beeps, almost like it was crying over its

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.310
own lack of tastebuds.

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:44.650
“I think that the problem here is obvious!”

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:48.030
a stuffier, older member of the board – Mr.
Omer – spoke up.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:52.540
“Look, we all know that the dang kids are
too distracted by their darn cell phones these

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:53.540
days.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.710
And sure, we could sit here and try to get
them interested in the anomalous entertainment

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.320
we’re trying to sell them.

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:04.400
Or we could try to profit off them by going
after what we know they’re already obsessed

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.400
with.”

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.851
“I really don’t think you should pursue
this train of thought, Mr. Omer,” an executive

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:11.980
named Mx, Clarron interjected.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.130
“No, this is a good idea!”

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.130
Omer protested.

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:16.130
“It’s simple.

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:19.150
We develop an app, a video game that the kids
won’t be able to get enough of.

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:23.380
But here’s the catch: in order to progress
in the game, we can encourage them to spend

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:28.690
real cash in exchange for some kind of virtual
currency that can only be used in the app

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:29.690
itself.

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.970
It’d essentially be worthless, useless even,
anywhere other than inside our game.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:36.630
But if they want to be the best at it, then
the kids will have to keep coughing over the

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:37.630
dough.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:41.860
And hey, we could even throw in some colonel-grade
spyware too, so when they have the app on

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.870
their phones, it simultaneously harvests all
their personal data and-”

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:49.460
At the snap of the doctor’s gloved fingers,
Mr Omer was suddenly vaporized, letting off

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.010
one final shriek as he turned into a pile
of ash on the table.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:58.990
“I think Mr. Omer failed to realize that
many other companies already beat us to the

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:04.090
punch with that idea,” Doctor Wondertainment
sighed, exasperated.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.580
“And besides, we aren’t monsters.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.480
Somebody else take the floor…

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:10.480
please.”

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:15.790
“W-well,” Mx Clarron mumbled, standing
up, wiping some of Mr. Omer’s ash off their

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:21.030
glasses, “Our social media team has been
doing some research into current online trends.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:24.980
And it turns out, we might have something
that fits with a really popular one.”

00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:30.280
Clarron produced a pristine doll’s house,
a to-scale recreation of a single-story home

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:31.889
in Albuquerque, New Mexico.

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:36.700
They opened up the walls, revealing a group
of figurines inside, each one moving of its

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:40.950
own accord, mimicking verbal speech with a
series of little noises.

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:46.040
Lifting off the house’s plastic roof, a
tiny, sculpted pizza slipped off the tiles.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:50.280
“So, we built this a while ago, during the
peak of the show’s popularity,” Clarron

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:51.280
explained.

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:55.070
“It’s a doll’s house, the dolls inside
are… somewhat alive, as you can see.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:58.720
But they’re always acting out the plot of
Breaking Bad, scene-by scene, in broadcast

00:06:58.720 --> 00:06:59.720
order.”

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:03.980
Sure enough, one of the dolls was a miniature,
bald-headed Walter White, making some little

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:08.100
squeaks that sounded like it was saying ‘I
am the one who knocks.’

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.230
“Breaking Bad has had a major resurgence
in meme culture lately, so this could be a

00:07:12.230 --> 00:07:14.740
viable product,” one of the other board
members chimed in.

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:16.650
“Right, exactly!”

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:17.650
Clarron smiled nervously.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:22.280
“Of course, we’d have to sort out a licensing
agreement with AMC… otherwise, we might

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:23.340
get sued…”

00:07:23.340 --> 00:07:26.560
Returning to their seat, Clarron trailed off,
hoping someone else would step in to fill

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.250
the latest bout of uncomfortable silence.

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:33.800
Wiping a few smears from his brow, a Mr. Ash
took the floor, his assistant wheeling in

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.310
a projector and hooking it up to a laptop.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.990
The rest of the executives couldn’t hear
the imaginary, building drum roll in Ash’s

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:42.860
head as he walked up and began to play a video
clip.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:47.100
“My fellow board members,” he announced
with a well-rehearsed beaming smile.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.419
“As… interesting as some of these pitches
have been, I believe I may well have something

00:07:52.419 --> 00:07:54.160
truly special to show you all.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.120
Behold: the Doctor Wondertainment Zoomatron
Pedal Car!”

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.639
Through the projector, the video showed a
large plastic toy car with a crash test dummy

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:03.710
sitting in it.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:07.800
It gradually began building speed at an impressive
rate for an outdoor toy.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:12.880
The Zoomatron was whipping around an indoor
testing track faster and faster until it was

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.830
little more than a blur, a speeding ring of
constant motion.

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:21.180
The board could just make out the dummy’s
arms flailing limply thanks to the sheer g-force.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:25.760
“I’m almost afraid to ask,” Mr. Glenridge
cleared his throat and spoke up.

00:08:25.760 --> 00:08:28.010
“But exactly how fast does that thing go.”

00:08:28.010 --> 00:08:30.450
“I’m very glad you asked!”

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:31.639
Mr. Ash replied excitedly.

00:08:31.639 --> 00:08:35.690
“It can reach up to Mach 6, with just an
average amount of pedals per second, or P.P.S.,

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:36.979
if you will.”

00:08:36.979 --> 00:08:38.680
“But surely that’s dangerous?”

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:39.870
Mx Clarron queried.

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:44.070
“I mean, an object of that size moving six
times the speed of sound that low to the ground?”

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:49.730
“I won’t deny, there have been a few…
let’s call them ‘teething problems’,”

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:50.800
Ash admitted.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:54.940
Almost on cue, the video showed a Wondertainment
Enterprises worker stepping accidentally into

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:59.390
the path of the speeding pedal car, only to
vanish in a split second as it whizzed by,

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:03.110
the car shot into, then through, a nearby
wall.

00:09:03.110 --> 00:09:06.560
“You have anything else, Mr. Ash?”

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.430
Doctor Wondertainment asked impatiently from
their chair.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:14.790
“I, uh, well I…” he stammered, rummaging
through some papers.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:19.320
“In a similar vein, we also developed an
electric scooter, you know, like those Bird

00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:22.000
ones you see people riding about on a lot.

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.460
Doesn’t quite go as fast as the Zoomatron,
so that could potentially be safer… marginally,

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.130
I meant to say marginally safer.”

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:31.620
“What’s the catch with this one?”

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.420
a board member asked, their exasperation obvious
in their tone.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:38.690
“It’s probably better if I just show you,”
Mr Ash sighed.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:42.960
He pressed play on another testing clip, wherein
a Wondertainment employee stood atop their

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:43.960
scooter.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.650
At first, it seemed to be moving normally;
that is, until the camera filming it zoomed

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:48.650
out.

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:51.450
The employee and the scooter were floating
several feet in the air,

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:55.560
“We’re, um, we’re still waiting for
that member of staff to return from their

00:09:55.560 --> 00:09:57.010
journey,” he said sheepishly.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:09:58.710
“I’ll just sit back down.”

00:09:58.710 --> 00:10:01.670
“What if we made a yo-yo out of lasagne?”

00:10:01.670 --> 00:10:02.670
Mr. Glenridge asked.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:06.990
The rest of the board all turned to look at
him confused, before he receded in his chair

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:11.650
out of embarrassment, fiddling with his starving
Tamagotchi in the hopes that the next outlandish

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:14.800
toy idea would make everyone forget what he
had just said.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:20.700
Luckily enough, one of the execs, Ms. Stevenson,
opened up her briefcase and produced a brightly-colored

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:21.700
stuffed bear.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:24.960
“If I might bring us back to basics,”
she announced.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.810
“Or ‘bear-sics’ if you’ll pardon the
pun-”

00:10:26.810 --> 00:10:30.700
“We won’t,” Doctor Wondertainment said
bluntly.

00:10:30.700 --> 00:10:34.700
“Oh… well, allow me to introduce the Doesn’t
Care Bear!”

00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.900
Stevenson forced a smile, then handed the
bear to the nearest board member.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.120
“What am I supposed to do with this?”

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:41.589
Mr. Ash asked, confused.

00:10:41.589 --> 00:10:45.700
“Just press the stomach,” Ms. Stevenson
urged, giving him a wide-eyed glare as if

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:49.450
to say either ‘work with me here’ or ‘please
help’.

00:10:49.450 --> 00:10:53.610
Doing as instructed, Ash gave the Doesn’t
Care Bear a gentle squeeze, activating a voice

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:55.570
box somewhere inside it.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:11:00.370
“Wow, Marlin, your surgeon really conned
you for those hair plugs,” the bear sneered

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:01.370
cruelly.

00:11:01.370 --> 00:11:06.550
Mr. Ash instinctively reached up to his forehead,
where his hairline had been noticeably receding

00:11:06.550 --> 00:11:10.750
until only a few months ago, but he’d never
told anyone about his transplant.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:14.000
In truth, it was something he felt pretty
insecure about.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:19.920
“I- I don’t know what it meant by that,”
Mr. Ash protested, playing it off with a half-hearted

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:20.920
chuckle.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:21.930
“Darn thing must be defective.”

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.370
“Not as defective as your scalp, Marlin!”

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:26.000
the bear gave another callous jeer.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:28.710
“Did you seriously think that looked realistic?

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:32.480
Nobody’s hairline is that ruler-straight,
not naturally anyway.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:35.730
Maybe if you’d been a baker like your dad
wanted you to, you could have avoided all

00:11:35.730 --> 00:11:38.210
the stress that made you lose your hair in
the first place.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:41.170
Then again, you were never that good of a
son to him anyway.”

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.100
“How in the hell do you turn this awful
thing off?”

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:46.649
Mr. Ash asked frantically, holding back tears.

00:11:46.649 --> 00:11:47.870
“Isn’t it great?”

00:11:47.870 --> 00:11:50.110
Ms. Stevenson said, forcing a toothy grin.

00:11:50.110 --> 00:11:53.320
“Imagine how great it would be for boosting
kids’ confidence!”

00:11:53.320 --> 00:11:56.170
“By honing in on their worst insecurities?”

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.470
Mx Clarron raised an eyebrow.

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:01.360
“Presenting them with something they don’t
like about themselves will challenge them

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.910
to improve on it,” Stevenson argued, in
an unjustly condescending voice.

00:12:05.910 --> 00:12:08.040
“That’s just basic reverse psychology.”

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.860
“Deborah, taking a one-day course doesn’t
make you a clinical psychiatrist,” the Doesn’t

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:14.340
Care Bear retorted in its cutesy voice.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.100
“You know, you really aren’t as smart
as you want people to think you are.”

00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:20.500
“How about a modern update of an old classic?”

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:24.910
Mr. Jefferies, an older executive spoke up,
excitedly setting up something on the table

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.020
in front of him.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:30.710
It was a pair of foldable playing boards,
with a collection of tiny ships, each one

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:32.279
a different size and shape.

00:12:32.279 --> 00:12:38.010
“Mx Clarron, if you’d be so kind,” he
said, passing them one half of the board game.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:40.150
“That’s just Battleship!”

00:12:40.150 --> 00:12:43.380
Stevenson retorted, stuffing the abrasive
bear back into her briefcase.

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.000
“Ah, but wait!”

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:48.950
Mr. Jeffries replied jovially, finishing placing
his miniature boats.

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:51.560
“Now, then, give me a co-ordinate.”

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:53.460
“Um, B6?”

00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:54.460
Clarron said.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:12:55.460
“Direct hit!”

00:12:55.460 --> 00:12:57.000
Jeffries clapped his hands excitedly.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:01.270
It was then that everyone noticed a tiny streak
of smoke shooting up from one of the ships

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:02.649
on Clarron’s board.

00:13:02.649 --> 00:13:06.940
Glowing, it soared directly upwards, leaving
a trail behind it, climbing through the air

00:13:06.940 --> 00:13:07.990
over the table.

00:13:07.990 --> 00:13:12.420
Then, once it was above Mr. Jeffries’ board,
the little glowing projectile began to plummet

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:13.690
straight back down.

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:18.060
At once, the board members all realized it
was a miniature missile, some of them urgently

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:21.100
ducking under their desks before it struck
its target.

00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:25.850
A small explosion erupted on Mr. Jeffries’
board, punctuated by a miniature fireball

00:13:25.850 --> 00:13:31.230
and cloud of black smoke that wafted up only
to be sucked into the boardroom’s air purifier.

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:35.830
From the battleship that had just been hit
with the shrunken ordinance, the Wondertainment

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:38.210
execs could hear some tiny, barely audible
voices.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:42.950
They were screaming and shouting in a state
of panic; ‘mayday, mayday’ and ‘abandon

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:47.399
ship, swim for your lives’ were the only
words anyone could clearly decipher.

00:13:47.399 --> 00:13:49.830
“Battleship: Extreme Edition!”

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:54.580
Mr. Jeffries announced proudly with a chuckle,
met with looks of concerned from the board

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:56.630
members brave enough to sit back in their
chairs.

00:13:56.630 --> 00:14:00.700
“While we’re on the topic of games,”
Mx Clarron spoke up again.

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:02.210
“I did have this.”

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:07.300
They produced a handheld video game player,
the kind that only featured one classic title.

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:12.170
Fortunately, the particular title was Pac-Man,
the distinctive music and flapping sound of

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:16.200
the titular character’s mouth instantly
recognizable even to the youngest members

00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:18.590
of the Wondertainment Enterprises board.

00:14:18.590 --> 00:14:21.540
“Come on, Kay,” one of the executives
sighed.

00:14:21.540 --> 00:14:23.540
“We’re all waiting to hear the catch.”

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:28.760
“It’s, um, this one is actually pretty
horrible,” they admitted, handing the game

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.970
across the table to Mr. Glenridge.

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:36.019
Seemingly happy to be free from the Tamagotchi’s
pleas for food, he began absent-mindedly playing

00:14:36.019 --> 00:14:37.019
Pac-Man.

00:14:37.019 --> 00:14:41.000
“I mean, it handles well,” he observed,
guiding the yellow, mouth-flapping icon around

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:45.290
the maze, avoiding multi-colored ghosts as
he chomped on any dots in his path.

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:48.730
“It’s quite a throwback too, eighties
nostalgia is still hot right now, isn’t

00:14:48.730 --> 00:14:49.730
it?

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:50.730
Oh, power capsule!”

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:54.470
Mr. Glenridge hit the corresponding buttons
to lead Pac-Man towards the larger dot, which

00:14:54.470 --> 00:14:58.820
granted him a power-up ability to eat the
ghosts that were patrolling the maze.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:03.040
Tapping away with both thumbs, he got Pac-Man
to follow in the path of one of the ghosts…

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:05.430
then the handheld game started screaming.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:06.430
“Billy!

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:07.990
Billy, please, no!”

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:10.100
shrieked a tinny, 8-bit voice.

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:11.100
“Ma?”

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.310
Mr. Glenridge’s face went pale, drained
of all color as he stared horrified at the

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:15.700
game in his hands.

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:19.070
Pac-Man kept following the path of the fleeing
ghost, gaining on it.

00:15:19.070 --> 00:15:21.740
The closer he got, the louder the compressed
screaming got.

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:22.740
“Billy, stop!

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:24.740
Don’t let it eat me, please, son!”

00:15:24.740 --> 00:15:29.280
But sure enough, Pac-Man caught up with the
ghost and kept up his perpetual chomping.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:33.820
As the ghost was eaten in the game, the built-in
speaker played the blood-curdling cries of

00:15:33.820 --> 00:15:37.290
Mr. Glenridge’s mother over the classic
theme music.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:42.310
Silently, with a look of pure shock on his
face, Glenridge put the game down on the desk.

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:46.000
“So, yeah, there’s that,” Clarron said
awkwardly.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:49.990
“A Pac-Man game with the souls of dead relatives
trapped inside.”

00:15:49.990 --> 00:15:54.950
“These toys are all… well, perfectly useless,
let’s be honest!”

00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:56.110
the good doctor erupted.

00:15:56.110 --> 00:16:00.839
“Are you all forgetting we have the SCP
Foundation breathing down our necks?

00:16:00.839 --> 00:16:04.560
Surely any one of these abominations would
have them poking around and investigating

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:05.700
us even more.”

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:08.279
“How about this:” one of the execs interrupted.

00:16:08.279 --> 00:16:10.490
“Picture Monopoly, but Site 19 edition.

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:15.209
We could really get our own back on the Foundation,
expose where their anomalies are kept, just

00:16:15.209 --> 00:16:16.769
generally cause some trouble for them.”

00:16:16.769 --> 00:16:19.910
“Oh, the playing pieces could be some of
the things they have in containment!”

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:20.910
another joined in.

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:25.520
“Instead of the shoe, the car, and the iron,
we might have… an anomalous shoe, car, and

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:26.520
iron.”

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.620
“And being sent to jail in the game would
turn a player into a member of their D Class

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:30.620
personnel!”

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:31.980
the first board member replied.

00:16:31.980 --> 00:16:32.980
“Enough!”

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:35.450
the doctor’s booming voice silenced the
whole room.

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:39.950
“That is… without a doubt, one of the
most absurdly stupid ideas I have ever heard

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.240
in the history of this company.”

00:16:42.240 --> 00:16:44.800
“With respect,” somebody spoke up.

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:46.870
“I think you’re all overthinking this.”

00:16:46.870 --> 00:16:50.470
One of the board members who had been sat
at the furthest end of the table, keeping

00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:54.740
quiet throughout the rest of the farcical
presentations, suddenly stood up.

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:58.230
Adjusting his tie, he addressed his fellow
Wondertainment executives.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:03.970
“Doctor, you’d like our – apologies,
your – company to uphold its core purpose,

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:07.739
to make toys and reach people all over the
globe,” he stated.

00:17:07.739 --> 00:17:11.569
“And, as a healthy added bonus, you’d
like to stick it to those killjoys over at

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:13.209
the SCP Foundation too.

00:17:13.209 --> 00:17:16.319
So, why don’t we do something that will
cover all those bases?”

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:19.189
“That’s what we’ve all been trying to
do!”

00:17:19.189 --> 00:17:20.189
Glenridge protested.

00:17:20.189 --> 00:17:23.730
“But, like I said, you’re overthinking
it,” the exec replied.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:25.689
“Let me show you all something.”

00:17:25.689 --> 00:17:28.109
Casually, he produced a stylized plush.

00:17:28.109 --> 00:17:33.499
The group leaned in, astonished to see it
resembled SCP-049, better known as the Plague

00:17:33.499 --> 00:17:34.499
Doctor.

00:17:34.499 --> 00:17:37.999
“We make plushies…” the board member
paused for dramatic effect.

00:17:37.999 --> 00:17:40.909
“Of the Foundation’s SCPs.”

00:17:40.909 --> 00:17:45.570
“Very intriguing,” Doctor Wondertainment
declared, finally turning their chair around

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:49.840
to face forward, examining the Plague Doctor
plush in their gloved hand.

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:55.330
Anyone who types that into their search engine
of choice will find high-quality plushies

00:17:55.330 --> 00:17:58.830
of some of the SCP Foundation’s most beloved
anomalies.

00:17:58.830 --> 00:18:03.471
There was a torturous pause; the members of
the board room could barely breathe, as Doctor

00:18:03.471 --> 00:18:05.210
Wondertainment rose to his feet…

00:18:05.210 --> 00:18:06.919
And began to clap.

00:18:06.919 --> 00:18:09.159
“You son of a bitch.

00:18:09.159 --> 00:18:10.450
I’m in.”

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:17.731
Now go and check out “SCP-001 A SIMPLE TOYMAKER”
and “ALL THE LITTLE MRS EXPLAINED”! for

00:18:17.731 --> 00:18:20.820
more of the wondrous world of Doctor Wondertainment!

